Kevin Wayne <killedbyafoo@yahoo.com> wrote:

>Quoth Philipp Lucas on this day of Tue, 04 May 2004 08:34:51 +0200:
>>Kevin Wayne <killedbyafoo@yahoo.com> wrote:
>>>Now, whenever I try "make clean /f makefile.bcc" or "make install /f
>>>makefile.bcc" I get the following error message:
>>>Error makefile 32: Unable to open include file 'win32.mak'
>>>*** 1 errors during make ***
>>This sounds like sys\winnt\Makefile.msc happened to be copied to
>>src\Makefile.bcc, instead of sys\winnt\Makefile.bcc. Rerun nhsetup.bat to
>>should put the right makefile in the src directory.
>Hmm. Didn't work. I know that the src/Makefile.bcc is in fact the
>Borland makefile--the text inside it makes that clear.

win32.mak is referenced only in Makefile.msc, and it is shipped with
Visual Studio, but not with the Borland tools. Maybe a recursive call of
make executes just src\Makefile (instead of src\Makefile.bcc), which
happens to be the VC makefile. Try compiling again after removing
src\Makefile and src\Makefile.msc. Additionally, many make-like
implementations have a command line switch that enables verbose
reportage, with which you can get a detailed account of what is
happening, and in particular, from where win32.mak is referenced.

